  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 140 PM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nAn active and rather complex forecast for the majority of the long \nterm period with poor model agreement on how multiple systems will \nmove across the Southeast US. The day with the highest confidence in \nthe forecast is (unsurprisingly) Monday, but even this has higher \nthan typical uncertainty for ~48 hours out as a decaying frontal \nboundary will start to push across the area out of the TN Valley.\nThe majority of model guidance keeps conditions dry across the area \nall the way through Tuesday, but a few models have moisture \nstreaming up into the area along the front and then having the front \nstall out, bringing rain chances from Monday night through Tuesday. \nLeaning towards the dry / low rain chance solutions given the weak \nforcing and dry air in place, but confidence in that is medium at \nbest. Regardless of which scenario plays out, high temperatures on \nMonday will be warm before the front moves through, topping out in \nthe upper 60s in North GA and low-mid 70s in Central GA.\n\nConfidence in the forecast drops off considerably from Tuesday night \nand on as a strong wave slides out of the Southern Plains across the \nGulf Coast on Wednesday. The track, speed, and strength of this \nsystem, as well as how it interacts with a wedge in place over \nGeorgia and another wave dropping from the northern Plains into the \nMidwest, will determine the weather in our area from mid to late \nweek. Unfortunately for us, model guidance continues to have \nsignificant spread on how the scenario will unfold. The only thing \nwe can say with any confidence is that these will create a period of \nmoist south-southwest flow over Georgia with widespread rain \nchances. The details of this, including how long the rain chances \nlast, where they are highest, and how intense the rainfall is, will \nbecome clearer in the coming days. The range of potential outcomes \nis large, with some solutions having the initial system move through \nquickly with little rainfall (0.1-0.25\" or less), while others on \nthe high end have a stronger system developing with a heavy rain and \ngusty wind threat for portions of our area. Despite all these \nuncertainties, the overall pattern will support a cooling trend, \nwith highs in the 50s to 60s and lows returning to the 30s by the \nend of the week. Stay tuned for updates, especially if you have \noutdoor plans for the Wed-Fri time period.",
